# TestFlow Core Product Rules

TestFlow is a web-based QA test management and browser automation platform.

## Phase 1 purpose
TestFlow Phase 1 allows a tester to define a specific test case or functionality, configure it, automate browser actions using Playwright record-and-playback, execute it, inspect the result, and manage published versions.

Primary workflow:
Project → Environment → Test Suite / Test Case → Configure Test Case → Arrange → Act → Assert → Record Playwright Actions → Review/Edit Generated Script → Run Test → View Result → Publish / Version Test Case

## Scope boundary
- Phase 1 tests explicitly defined test cases and functionalities.
- Phase 1 is NOT an autonomous whole-website crawler or whole-site testing system.
- A base URL is configuration, not an instruction to discover or test the entire website.
- Do not add whole-site crawling, autonomous page discovery, AI-generated testing, CI/CD functionality, or future-phase features unless explicitly requested.

## Core model
- Project: application or website under test.
- Environment: configuration such as a base URL.
- Test Case: one specific functionality or behavior to test.
- Test Suite: organization/grouping of test cases.
- Preserve existing many-to-many suite membership where implemented.

### Arrange
Setup/preconditions, environment selection, relative/start path, and applicable test configuration/data.

### Act
Browser interactions through Playwright record-and-playback and the generated Playwright script. Preserve script viewing/editing where supported.

### Assert
Defines the expected result. Keep assertion behavior consistent with the existing implementation unless explicitly changed.

### Result
Shows execution outcome and relevant execution information.

## URL handling
Use the existing environment-aware relative URL mechanism:
Environment Base URL + Test Case Start Path = Resolved Test URL.
Do not replace this with hardcoded URLs when the existing resolver applies.

## Versioning
- Test cases support draft and published states.
- Publishing creates a version snapshot.
- Changes after publishing may create a new draft and later another published version.
- Preserve the existing versioning model unless explicitly asked to change it.

## Product philosophy
Keep TestFlow practical, minimal, understandable, and focused on QA workflows.
Do not over-engineer. Implement the requested requirement only.
